/* ================= Practice Test 4 · Reading and Writing =================
   Module 1: mixed difficulty. Module 2: an easier (m2e) and a harder (m2h) version.
   Each module: 27 questions, in the official order of question types.
   Researchers, authors and studies named here are fictional unless a fact is common knowledge. */
testSet('t4', 'RW', 'm1', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'The volunteers worked all morning to ______ the beach, filling bags with plastic and other trash that visitors had left behind.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['clean', 'crowd', 'measure', 'paint'], a: 0, x: 'Filling bags with trash means they were working to **clean** the beach.', w: ['', 'Collecting trash doesn’t crowd a beach.', 'Collecting trash isn’t measuring.', 'Nothing about painting is mentioned; they collected trash.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'Unlike his outgoing older sister, Theo was ______, preferring to spend his lunch break reading alone in the library.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['reserved', 'boisterous', 'generous', 'careless'], a: 0, x: '“Unlike” his outgoing sister, Theo likes being alone and quiet: he is **reserved**.', w: ['', '“Boisterous” means loud and lively, like his sister, not the opposite.', 'Generosity has nothing to do with being outgoing or quiet.', 'Nothing suggests he is careless; the contrast is about being outgoing.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'Recent fossil discoveries **bear out** the long-held idea that birds descended from small, feathered dinosaurs.', p: 'As used in the text, what does the phrase “bear out” most nearly mean?', o: ['confirm', 'carry away', 'tolerate', 'give birth to'], a: 0, x: 'Fossils that support an existing idea **confirm** it.', w: ['', 'Discoveries can’t carry an idea away; “bear out” means support.', '“Tolerate” is the meaning in “I can’t bear it”, which doesn’t fit evidence.', '“Give birth to” is another meaning of “bear”; the idea already existed.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'Critics praised the documentary’s ______ approach: rather than telling viewers what to think, it presented interviews with people on every side of the dispute and let viewers draw their own conclusions.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['evenhanded', 'polemical', 'sensational', 'perfunctory'], a: 0, x: 'Giving every side a voice without pushing a conclusion is fair and balanced: **evenhanded**.', w: ['', '“Polemical” means strongly arguing one side, the opposite of letting viewers decide.', '“Sensational” means designed to shock, which doesn’t fit a fair presentation of all sides.', '“Perfunctory” means careless or minimal, but the film was praised for its thoughtful approach.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 1, ps: 'The giant panda’s diet is made up almost entirely of bamboo. Because bamboo provides little nutrition, a panda must eat a great deal of it, spending up to 14 hours a day feeding and consuming as much as 38 kilograms in a single day.', p: Q_PURP, o: ['To explain why giant pandas spend so much of each day eating', 'To argue that bamboo forests should be expanded', 'To compare giant pandas with other kinds of bears', 'To describe how bamboo plants grow'], a: 0, x: 'The text links the panda’s low-nutrition diet to its long hours of eating.', w: ['', 'The text doesn’t argue for anything.', 'Other bears aren’t mentioned.', 'How bamboo grows isn’t described.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 2, ps: 'Builders of medieval Gothic cathedrals wanted walls filled with tall stained-glass windows. ==But walls with so many openings were too weak to hold up the heavy stone roofs on their own.== Builders solved the problem with flying buttresses: stone arches outside the building that carried part of the roof’s weight down to the ground.', p: Q_FUNC, o: ['It identifies a problem that the text then explains how builders solved.', 'It explains why the builders wanted stained-glass windows.', 'It describes the materials used to make flying buttresses.', 'It argues that Gothic cathedrals were poorly designed.'], a: 0, x: 'The underlined sentence states the problem (weak walls), and the next sentence gives the solution (flying buttresses).', w: ['', 'The first sentence says they wanted the windows; the underlined sentence describes the problem the windows caused.', 'Flying buttresses are described in the next sentence, not this one.', 'The text shows builders solving the problem, not that the cathedrals were badly designed.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 3, ps: '**Text 1**\nSome economists argue that people save too little for retirement mainly because they lack financial knowledge. Teaching people how savings grow over time, they argue, should lead them to save more.\n\n**Text 2**\nIn a study at one large company, workers who took a financial-education course knew much more about saving afterward, but their savings rose only slightly. When the same company began enrolling new workers in its retirement plan automatically while letting them opt out, participation jumped from about 40 percent to more than 85 percent.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the argument in Text 1?', o: ['By suggesting that a lack of knowledge may not be the main obstacle to saving, since changing the default option had a far larger effect than education did', 'By agreeing that financial education is the most effective way to increase saving', 'By arguing that workers should not be allowed to opt out of retirement plans', 'By claiming that the financial-education course did not increase workers’ knowledge'], a: 0, x: 'In Text 2, education raised knowledge but barely changed saving, while automatic enrollment changed it a lot, so knowledge seems not to be the main barrier.', w: ['', 'Text 2 found education had only a slight effect on saving.', 'Text 2 reports what happened with opting out allowed; it doesn’t argue against opting out.', 'Text 2 says workers knew much more after the course.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 1, ps: 'Venus spins very slowly: one day on Venus, measured from one sunrise to the next, lasts about 117 Earth days. Venus also spins in the opposite direction from most planets, so on Venus the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east.', p: 'According to the text, why does the Sun rise in the west on Venus?', o: ['Venus spins in the opposite direction from most planets.', 'A day on Venus lasts about 117 Earth days.', 'Venus is closer to the Sun than Earth is.', 'Venus spins very slowly.'], a: 0, x: 'The text links the western sunrise to Venus spinning “in the opposite direction from most planets”.', w: ['', 'The length of a day doesn’t explain which direction the Sun rises in.', 'Distance from the Sun isn’t mentioned.', 'Slow spinning explains the long day, not the direction of sunrise.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 2, ps: 'In the 1920s, the Harlem neighborhood of New York City became a center of Black American art, music and writing. Poets such as Langston Hughes and novelists such as Zora Neale Hurston published widely read works, and jazz musicians filled the neighborhood’s clubs. The movement, later called the Harlem Renaissance, shaped American culture for decades afterward.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['In the 1920s, Harlem was the center of a creative movement that had a lasting influence on American culture.', 'Langston Hughes was the most important writer of the Harlem Renaissance.', 'Jazz was invented in Harlem during the 1920s.', 'Most writers of the Harlem Renaissance were novelists.'], a: 0, x: 'The text describes the art, music and writing of 1920s Harlem and ends by noting its long influence.', w: ['', 'Hughes is one example; the text doesn’t rank writers.', 'The text says jazz musicians played in Harlem’s clubs, not that jazz began there.', 'The text names a poet and a novelist and doesn’t say which kind of writer was more common.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 2, ps: 'Psychologist Amir Sadeghi claims that people remember information better after explaining it to someone else than after simply rereading it.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support Sadeghi’s claim?', o: ['Students who explained a lesson to a classmate scored higher on a test about it a week later than students who spent the same time rereading the lesson.', 'Students said they found rereading easier than explaining.', 'Students who reread the lesson finished their study sessions more quickly.', 'Students who explained the lesson and students who reread it scored about the same on the test.'], a: 0, x: 'Better test scores for explainers, with study time held equal, directly support the claim.', w: ['', 'What students found easier says nothing about how well they remembered.', 'Speed says nothing about how well they remembered.', 'Equal scores would weaken the claim, not support it.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'In a novel, the narrator describes her grandfather as someone whose stern appearance hid a playful sense of humor.', p: 'Which quotation from the novel most effectively illustrates this claim?', o: ['“He rarely smiled for photographs, and his eyebrows alone could silence a room, yet he hid sweets in his coat pockets and invented riddles for every child he met.”', '“He laughed constantly and loved to joke with everyone he knew.”', '“His stern face frightened the neighborhood children.”', '“He worked as a judge in the county court for thirty years.”'], a: 0, x: 'The claim has two parts. Only this quotation shows the stern appearance (no smiles, silencing eyebrows) and the hidden playfulness (sweets and riddles).', w: ['', 'This shows humor, but not a stern appearance hiding it.', 'This shows sternness, but not the playful humor.', 'His job shows neither sternness nor humor.'] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 3, ps: 'A student researching transportation in the town of Riverton notes that the share of commuters who bike rose every year from 2018 to 2023, while the share who drove alone fell every year except from 2019 to 2020, when it ______', ch: { t: 'line', title: 'Riverton commuters (%)', x: ['2018', '2019', '2020', '2021', '2022', '2023'], s: [{ n: 'Drove alone', v: [72, 70, 71, 66, 64, 61] }, { n: 'Biked', v: [3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10] }] }, p: Q_GRAPH, o: ['rose from 70% to 71%.', 'fell from 72% to 70%.', 'fell from 71% to 66%.', 'rose from 4% to 5%.'], a: 0, x: 'The exception is the driving share from 2019 (70%) to 2020 (71%), the only year it went up.', w: ['', 'That’s the change from 2018 to 2019, and it was a fall, not the exception.', 'That’s the change from 2020 to 2021, which was a fall.', 'Those numbers are for biking, not driving alone.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 2, ps: 'Researchers placed identical bird feeders in 20 backyards. Ten feeders were near windows covered with small stickers that make glass easier for birds to see; ten were near plain windows. Over one month, birds flew into the plain windows about five times as often as into the windows with stickers. This suggests that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['making windows more visible to birds may reduce collisions.', 'birds prefer feeders near plain windows.', 'stickers attract more birds to feeders.', 'birds cannot see windows of any kind.'], a: 0, x: 'The feeders were identical; only the windows differed, and windows with stickers had far fewer collisions.', w: ['', 'The study counted collisions, not which feeders birds preferred.', 'Nothing suggests stickers drew more birds; they reduced collisions.', 'Birds hit the windows with stickers much less often, so they could see those windows.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'A historian studying the letters of a 17th-century merchant notes that the merchant wrote often to trading partners in Amsterdam and Lisbon, but that no letters to or from partners in London survive. The merchant’s account books, however, record regular payments to a London trading house throughout the same years. The historian concludes that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['the merchant probably did correspond with partners in London, but those letters have not survived.', 'the merchant had no business dealings in London.', 'the account books are less reliable than the letters.', 'the merchant’s partners in London could not write.'], a: 0, x: 'Regular payments show an ongoing business relationship with London, so the missing letters were probably lost, not never written.', w: ['', 'The account books record regular payments to a London trading house.', 'Nothing suggests the account books are unreliable; they fill a gap in the letters.', 'Nothing suggests the London partners couldn’t write; their letters may simply be lost.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'When the power went ______ the students finished their experiment by flashlight.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['out,', 'out;', 'out:', 'out'], a: 0, x: 'An opening “When…” clause is followed by a comma.', w: ['', 'A semicolon needs a complete sentence before it, but the “When…” clause isn’t one.', 'A colon needs a complete sentence before it.', 'Without a comma, “out the students” runs together.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The violinist’s performance was ______ every note was clear, and her timing never slipped.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['flawless:', 'flawless,', 'flawless', 'flawless, which'], a: 0, x: 'A complete sentence comes first, and what follows explains why the performance was flawless, so a colon fits.', w: ['', 'A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together.', '“Which every note was clear” isn’t grammatical.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'Some of the oldest known musical ______ flutes carved from bird bones and mammoth ivory—were found in caves in southern Germany and are more than 35,000 years old.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['instruments—', 'instruments,', 'instruments:', 'instruments'], a: 0, x: 'The description of the instruments closes with a dash before “were”, so it must open with a dash.', w: ['', 'The description closes with a dash, so it must open with one, not a comma.', 'The description closes with a dash, so it must open with one; a colon also can’t split a subject from its verb.', 'Without opening punctuation, the description runs into “instruments”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The idea that the universe began in a hot, dense state and has been expanding ever ______ is supported by observations of faint radiation left over from the universe’s earliest moments.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['since', 'since,', 'since;', 'since—'], a: 0, x: 'Everything before “is” is one long subject (“The idea that…”), and no punctuation goes between a subject and its verb.', w: ['', 'A comma here separates the long subject from its verb, “is”.', 'A semicolon here separates the subject from its verb, leaving two fragments.', 'A single dash here separates the subject from its verb.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'My cousins ______ to visit us every summer.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['come', 'comes', 'is coming', 'has come'], a: 0, x: '“Cousins” is plural, so the verb is “come”.', w: ['', '“Comes” is singular, but “cousins” is plural.', '“Is coming” is singular, but “cousins” is plural.', '“Has come” is singular, but “cousins” is plural.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'Known for its bright red feathers, ______', p: Q_SEC, o: ['the northern cardinal is one of the most recognizable birds in North America.', 'many people in North America easily recognize the northern cardinal.', 'the northern cardinal’s bright red feathers make it easy to recognize.', 'North America is home to the northern cardinal.'], a: 0, x: 'The thing known for its red feathers is the northern cardinal, so it must come right after the comma.', w: ['', 'This makes it sound like the people are known for bright red feathers.', 'This makes it sound like the feathers are known for their own feathers.', 'This makes it sound like North America is known for bright red feathers.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'Neither of the two companies has released ______ quarterly earnings report yet.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['its', 'their', 'it’s', 'they’re'], a: 0, x: '“Neither” is singular (as “has released” shows), so the possessive pronoun is “its”.', w: ['', '“Neither” is singular, so “their” doesn’t agree.', '“It’s” means “it is”.', '“They’re” means “they are”.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'The chef wanted her soup to taste fresher. ______ she stirred in a handful of chopped herbs just before serving it.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Therefore,', 'However,', 'For example,', 'In contrast,'], a: 0, x: 'Adding herbs is what she did because she wanted fresher taste: cause and effect.', w: ['', 'Adding herbs doesn’t contrast with wanting fresher taste; it follows from it.', 'Adding herbs isn’t an example of wanting fresher taste; it’s her response to it.', 'There is no contrast between the two sentences.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'Mercury is the planet closest to the Sun. ______ it is not the hottest planet; Venus, whose thick atmosphere traps heat, is hotter.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['However,', 'Therefore,', 'In addition,', 'Similarly,'], a: 0, x: 'You’d expect the closest planet to be hottest, but it isn’t: a contrast.', w: ['', 'Not being the hottest isn’t a result of being closest; it’s surprising given that fact.', '“In addition” adds a similar point, but this one goes against what you’d expect.', 'Not being the hottest isn’t similar to being closest to the Sun.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'The town council expected the new park to be used mainly by families with young children. ______ its most frequent visitors turned out to be older adults, who came each morning for the shaded walking paths.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['In practice,', 'Likewise,', 'Specifically,', 'For this reason,'], a: 0, x: 'The second sentence describes what actually happened, which differed from the expectation: “In practice”.', w: ['', 'Older adults being the main visitors isn’t similar to the expectation; it differs from it.', '“Specifically” would add detail about families with children, but the sentence is about older adults.', 'Older adults weren’t the main visitors because of the council’s expectation.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 1, ps: NOTES + '- Lake Baikal is in Siberia, in Russia.\n- It is the deepest lake in the world.\n- At its deepest point, it is about 1,640 meters deep.\n- It holds about one-fifth of the world’s unfrozen fresh surface water.', p: 'The student wants to state how deep Lake Baikal is. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['At its deepest point, Lake Baikal is about 1,640 meters deep.', 'Lake Baikal is in Siberia, in Russia.', 'Lake Baikal holds about one-fifth of the world’s unfrozen fresh surface water.', 'Lake Baikal is a lake in Russia that holds a great deal of water.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice gives the lake’s depth.', w: ['', 'This gives the location, not the depth.', 'This is about how much water it holds, not its depth.', 'This is about location and amount of water, not depth.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- The kākāpō is a flightless parrot found only in New Zealand.\n- It is the heaviest parrot in the world.\n- It is active mainly at night.\n- Fewer than 300 kākāpō are alive today, and conservation workers monitor every one of them.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize how rare the kākāpō is. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['With fewer than 300 alive today, each one monitored by conservation workers, the kākāpō is extremely rare.', 'The kākāpō is the heaviest parrot in the world.', 'The kākāpō is a flightless parrot that is active mainly at night.', 'The kākāpō is a parrot that cannot fly.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice tells how few kākāpō exist.', w: ['', 'This is about the bird’s weight, not how rare it is.', 'This describes the bird’s habits, not how rare it is.', 'This describes the bird, not how rare it is.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- Engineers want to protect coastal towns from rising seas.\n- One approach is to build sea walls, which block waves.\n- Sea walls can speed up the erosion of nearby beaches.\n- Another approach is to restore salt marshes, which absorb wave energy and can grow upward as sea levels rise.\n- A restored salt marsh can take years to provide much protection.', p: 'The student wants to present a drawback of each approach. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Sea walls can speed up the erosion of nearby beaches, while restored salt marshes can take years to provide much protection.', 'Sea walls block waves, while salt marshes absorb wave energy.', 'Restored salt marshes can grow upward as sea levels rise.', 'Engineers want to protect coastal towns from rising seas.'], a: 0, x: 'The goal asks for a drawback of each approach; only one choice gives both drawbacks.', w: ['', 'These are benefits of each approach, not drawbacks.', 'This is a benefit of salt marshes, and sea walls aren’t mentioned.', 'This states the goal of both approaches, not their drawbacks.'] }
]);
testSet('t4', 'RW', 'm2e', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'The kitten was so ______ that it fit easily in the palm of the veterinarian’s hand.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['small', 'loud', 'heavy', 'old'], a: 0, x: 'Fitting in the palm of a hand means the kitten was **small**.', w: ['', 'Being loud doesn’t explain fitting in a hand.', 'A heavy kitten wouldn’t fit easily in a hand.', 'Age doesn’t explain fitting in a hand.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'The firefighters acted ______, reaching the burning house within minutes of the call.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['quickly', 'slowly', 'quietly', 'rarely'], a: 0, x: 'Arriving within minutes means they acted **quickly**.', w: ['', 'Arriving within minutes is the opposite of acting slowly.', 'Being quiet doesn’t explain how fast they arrived.', '“Rarely” means not often, which doesn’t fit a single fast response.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The author’s second book was even more ______ than her first: it sold twice as many copies in its first month.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['successful', 'obscure', 'difficult', 'brief'], a: 0, x: 'Selling twice as many copies shows the book was more **successful**.', w: ['', '“Obscure” means little known, but the book sold very well.', 'Strong sales don’t show that a book is difficult.', 'Length isn’t mentioned; the clue is sales.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The company plans to **launch** its new phone in the spring, with a large event to introduce it to the public.', p: Q_MEAN('launch'), o: ['release', 'throw', 'fire', 'sail'], a: 0, x: 'Introducing a new phone to the public means making it available: to **release** it.', w: ['', '“Throw” is a physical meaning of “launch”, not what a company does with a new product.', '“Fire” is the meaning for rockets or missiles.', '“Sail” is the meaning for boats.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 1, ps: 'Polar bears have black skin underneath their thick white fur. The dark skin absorbs heat from sunlight, while the fur traps a layer of warm air close to the body. Together, these features help polar bears stay warm in the bitter Arctic cold.', p: Q_PURP, o: ['To explain how polar bears’ skin and fur help them stay warm', 'To describe what polar bears eat', 'To compare polar bears with brown bears', 'To argue that the Arctic should be protected'], a: 0, x: 'The text explains what the skin and the fur each do and says that together they keep polar bears warm.', w: ['', 'Food isn’t mentioned.', 'Brown bears aren’t mentioned.', 'The text doesn’t argue for anything.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 2, ps: 'In the early 1900s, most American homes were lit by gas lamps, oil lamps or candles. As power lines spread through cities and then the countryside over the following decades, electric lighting became more and more common. By the 1950s, most homes in the United States had electric lights.', p: Q_STRUCT, o: ['It traces how the way homes were lit changed over several decades.', 'It explains in detail how gas lamps work.', 'It argues that electric lights are safer than candles.', 'It compares how homes were lit in two different countries.'], a: 0, x: 'The text moves in time order from lamps and candles in the early 1900s to electric lights by the 1950s.', w: ['', 'Gas lamps are mentioned but not explained.', 'Safety isn’t discussed.', 'Only the United States is discussed.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 2, ps: '**Text 1**\nMany gardeners believe that watering plants a little every day is the best way to keep them healthy.\n\n**Text 2**\nHorticulturists point out that frequent, shallow watering keeps plant roots near the surface, where the soil dries out quickly. Watering deeply but less often encourages roots to grow downward, which helps plants survive dry spells.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the horticulturists in Text 2 most likely respond to the belief described in Text 1?', o: ['By arguing that watering deeply but less often may be better for plants than watering a little every day', 'By agreeing that plants should be watered a little every day', 'By claiming that plants don’t need water during dry spells', 'By suggesting that roots grow best near the surface of the soil'], a: 0, x: 'Text 2 says daily shallow watering keeps roots shallow and recommends deep, less frequent watering instead.', w: ['', 'Text 2 points out a problem with watering a little every day.', 'Text 2 is about helping plants survive dry spells, not skipping water.', 'Text 2 says shallow roots are a problem because the surface dries out quickly.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 1, ps: 'Koalas sleep for up to 20 hours a day. Their diet of eucalyptus leaves is low in energy and hard to digest, so sleeping for long periods helps them save energy.', p: 'According to the text, why do koalas sleep so much?', o: ['Their diet gives them little energy, so sleep helps them save it.', 'They are active only during heavy rain.', 'They need to hide from predators during the day.', 'Eucalyptus leaves contain a chemical that makes them sleepy.'], a: 0, x: 'The text says their food is low in energy and hard to digest, so long sleep saves energy.', w: ['', 'Rain isn’t mentioned.', 'Predators aren’t mentioned.', 'The text says the leaves are low in energy; it doesn’t mention a chemical.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 2, ps: 'Ana had planned to spend her first day at the new school without being noticed. But when the teacher asked who could explain the math problem on the board and the room fell silent, Ana, almost before she knew it, raised her hand.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['Despite planning to stay unnoticed, Ana volunteers to answer a question on her first day.', 'Ana dislikes math and tries to avoid it.', 'Ana’s teacher calls on her because she is new.', 'Ana is upset that no one else knows the answer.'], a: 0, x: 'The text sets up Ana’s plan to go unnoticed and then shows her raising her hand anyway.', w: ['', 'She volunteers to explain a math problem, so she doesn’t avoid math.', 'Ana raises her hand herself; the teacher doesn’t single her out.', 'Nothing describes her feelings about the silence.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 1, ps: 'In a short story, the narrator describes a storm as frightening.', p: 'Which quotation from the story most effectively illustrates this claim?', o: ['“Thunder shook the windows, and each flash of lightning showed the trees bending as if they might snap.”', '“The storm began on a Tuesday afternoon.”', '“Afterward, the air smelled fresh and clean.”', '“My brother and I played cards by the fire.”'], a: 0, x: 'Shaking windows and trees about to snap make the storm frightening.', w: ['', 'A start time isn’t frightening.', 'Fresh air after the storm is pleasant, not frightening.', 'Playing cards by the fire is a calm, cozy image.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 2, ps: 'A student claims that her school’s new, later start time caused students to arrive late less often.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the student’s claim?', o: ['The number of late arrivals was about the same in the year before and the year after the start time changed.', 'Many students said they liked the later start time.', 'The school buses arrived at the same time each morning.', 'Late arrivals fell by half after the start time changed.'], a: 0, x: 'If lateness didn’t change, the new start time didn’t reduce it.', w: ['', 'Liking the start time doesn’t show whether lateness changed.', 'Bus timing doesn’t say whether lateness changed.', 'A drop in late arrivals would support the claim, not weaken it.'] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 1, ps: 'A class counted the birds that visited a feeder each school day for one week. The most birds visited on Thursday: ______', ch: { t: 'table', title: 'Birds seen at the feeder', head: ['Day', 'Birds'], rows: [['Monday', '12'], ['Tuesday', '9'], ['Wednesday', '15'], ['Thursday', '22'], ['Friday', '18']] }, p: Q_DATA, o: ['22 birds visited on Thursday, more than on any other day.', '18 birds visited on Friday.', 'more birds visited on Wednesday than on Tuesday.', '12 birds visited on Monday.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice shows Thursday had the most birds.', w: ['', 'This is about Friday, not Thursday.', 'This compares Wednesday and Tuesday, not Thursday.', 'This is about Monday, not Thursday.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 1, ps: 'Plants need sunlight to make their food. A gardener put one pot of basil on a sunny windowsill and an identical pot in a dark closet. After two weeks, the basil in the closet had most likely ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['grown much less than the basil on the windowsill.', 'grown taller and greener than the basil on the windowsill.', 'produced more leaves than the other plant.', 'stayed exactly the same as the basil on the windowsill.'], a: 0, x: 'Without sunlight, the closet plant can’t make much food, so it would grow less.', w: ['', 'Without sunlight, the plant can’t make the food it needs to grow well.', 'The plant in the dark can’t make much food, so it wouldn’t produce more leaves.', 'The two pots got very different amounts of light, so they wouldn’t grow the same.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 2, ps: 'Many residents of a town threw away their food scraps. The town gave every household a free compost bin and simple instructions. The next year, the amount of trash collected from homes fell by 15 percent, though the number of households stayed the same. This suggests that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['many residents began composting food scraps instead of throwing them away.', 'fewer people lived in the town that year.', 'residents stopped eating meals at home.', 'the compost bins were too small to be useful.'], a: 0, x: 'Trash fell after compost bins were handed out, while the number of households stayed the same, so residents probably composted their scraps.', w: ['', 'The number of households stayed the same.', 'Nothing suggests people stopped eating at home.', 'Trash fell after the bins arrived, which suggests they were used.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'Our team won the first ______ we lost the second one by a single point.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['game, but', 'game but,', 'game,', 'game, but,'], a: 0, x: 'A comma plus “but” joins two complete sentences. No comma goes after “but”.', w: ['', 'The comma belongs before “but”, not after it.', 'A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'No comma belongs after “but”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'If it rains ______ the outdoor concert will move to the school gym.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['tomorrow,', 'tomorrow;', 'tomorrow:', 'tomorrow'], a: 0, x: 'An opening “If…” clause is followed by a comma.', w: ['', 'A semicolon needs a complete sentence before it, but the “If…” clause isn’t one.', 'A colon needs a complete sentence before it.', 'Without a comma, “tomorrow the outdoor concert” runs together.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'Elephants communicate using low rumbling ______ some of these rumbles are too deep for humans to hear.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['sounds;', 'sounds,', 'sounds', 'sounds, and,'], a: 0, x: 'Both parts are complete sentences, so a semicolon joins them.', w: ['', 'A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together.', 'No comma belongs after “and”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The Amazon rainforest, which spans parts of nine ______ is the largest tropical rainforest in the world.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['countries,', 'countries', 'countries;', 'countries:'], a: 0, x: 'The “which” clause opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma before the verb “is”.', w: ['', 'The clause opens with a comma, so it needs a closing comma before “is”.', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “is”.', 'A colon would cut the subject off from its verb, “is”.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'Last weekend, my family ______ a picnic by the lake.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['had', 'has', 'will have', 'is having'], a: 0, x: '“Last weekend” is in the past, so the past tense “had” fits.', w: ['', '“Has” is present tense, but “last weekend” is in the past.', '“Will have” is future tense, but “last weekend” is in the past.', '“Is having” is present tense, but “last weekend” is in the past.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'The birds in the old oak tree ______ loudly every morning.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['sing', 'sings', 'is singing', 'has sung'], a: 0, x: 'The subject is “birds” (plural), so the verb is “sing”.', w: ['', '“Sings” is singular, but “birds” is plural.', '“Is singing” is singular, but “birds” is plural.', '“Has sung” is singular, but “birds” is plural.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'Every one of the cookies on the plate ______ eaten before lunch.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['was', 'were', 'are', 'have been'], a: 0, x: '“Every one” is singular, so the verb is “was”.', w: ['', '“Were” is plural, but “every one” is singular.', '“Are” is plural and present tense, but “every one” is singular.', '“Have been” is plural, but “every one” is singular.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'Snakes do not have eyelids. ______ they cannot blink.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['As a result,', 'However,', 'For example,', 'Similarly,'], a: 0, x: 'Not being able to blink is a result of having no eyelids.', w: ['', 'Not blinking doesn’t contrast with having no eyelids; it follows from it.', 'Not blinking isn’t an example of having no eyelids; it’s a result.', 'Not blinking isn’t similar to having no eyelids; it’s caused by it.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'First, mix the flour and sugar together in a large bowl. ______ add the eggs and stir until the batter is smooth.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Then,', 'However,', 'For example,', 'In contrast,'], a: 0, x: 'Adding the eggs is the next step in the recipe.', w: ['', 'The next step doesn’t contrast with the first.', 'Adding eggs isn’t an example of mixing flour and sugar.', 'The steps don’t contrast; one follows the other.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'The library’s summer reading program was very popular with young children. ______ few teenagers signed up, so the library created a separate program for older readers.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['However,', 'Therefore,', 'Similarly,', 'For instance,'], a: 0, x: 'Popular with children but not with teenagers: a contrast.', w: ['', 'Few teenagers signing up isn’t a result of the program being popular with children.', 'Teenagers didn’t respond the same way as children, so the ideas aren’t similar.', 'Teenagers not signing up isn’t an example of the program’s popularity.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 1, ps: NOTES + '- The cheetah is the fastest land animal.\n- It can reach speeds of about 100 kilometers per hour.\n- It lives mainly in Africa.', p: 'The student wants to state how fast a cheetah can run. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The cheetah can reach speeds of about 100 kilometers per hour.', 'The cheetah lives mainly in Africa.', 'The cheetah is an animal that lives on land.', 'Cheetahs are found in Africa.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice gives the cheetah’s speed.', w: ['', 'This gives where it lives, not its speed.', 'This doesn’t mention speed.', 'This gives where it lives, not its speed.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- Mars and Earth both have seasons.\n- A year on Earth lasts 365 days.\n- A year on Mars lasts about 687 Earth days.\n- Because the Martian year is longer, each season on Mars lasts much longer than a season on Earth.', p: 'The student wants to explain why seasons on Mars last longer than seasons on Earth. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Because a year on Mars lasts about 687 Earth days, nearly twice as long as Earth’s 365, each Martian season lasts much longer than a season on Earth.', 'Mars and Earth both have seasons.', 'A year on Earth lasts 365 days.', 'Mars is a planet that has seasons.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice gives the reason: the longer Martian year.', w: ['', 'This is a similarity and gives no reason.', 'This is about Earth only and gives no reason.', 'This says Mars has seasons but not why they’re longer.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- A school tested whether a 10-minute morning stretch improves students’ focus.\n- For one month, one class stretched every morning; another class did not.\n- Teachers rated the class that stretched as more focused.', p: 'The student wants to describe the study’s method. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['For one month, one class stretched for 10 minutes every morning while another class did not.', 'Teachers rated the class that stretched as more focused.', 'The school wanted to know whether stretching improves focus.', 'Stretching in the morning is good for students.'], a: 0, x: 'The method is what the school did; only one choice describes it.', w: ['', 'This is the result, not the method.', 'This is the question the study asked, not its method.', 'This is a broad conclusion, not the method.'] }
]);
testSet('t4', 'RW', 'm2h', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'Though the town’s surviving records are ______, covering only scattered years and omitting several key decades, historians have pieced together a rough account of how the town grew.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['fragmentary', 'exhaustive', 'fabricated', 'redundant'], a: 0, x: 'Records covering only scattered years, with decades missing, are incomplete: **fragmentary**.', w: ['', '“Exhaustive” means complete and thorough, the opposite of records with whole decades missing.', 'Nothing suggests the records were faked; they are incomplete.', '“Redundant” means repetitive, but the problem is gaps, not repetition.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The board’s approval of the plan was ______: members voted in favor of it but attached so many conditions that the plan, as written, could hardly go forward.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['nominal', 'wholehearted', 'irrevocable', 'spontaneous'], a: 0, x: 'The board approved the plan in name only, since its conditions made the plan nearly impossible: the approval was **nominal**.', w: ['', '“Wholehearted” means full and enthusiastic, but the conditions undercut the approval.', '“Irrevocable” means unable to be undone, which isn’t the point; the point is that the approval meant little.', '“Spontaneous” means unplanned, but attaching many conditions is deliberate.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The professor’s comments on the proposal were **pointed**: she named each of its weaknesses directly and offered no softening praise.', p: Q_MEAN('pointed'), o: ['sharply critical', 'sharp at the tip', 'carefully aimed at a target', 'shown with a gesture'], a: 0, x: 'Naming every weakness directly with no softening is **sharply critical**.', w: ['', 'That’s the physical meaning of “pointed” (like a pencil), not a meaning for comments.', 'This literal sense of aiming doesn’t describe the tone of the comments.', 'This is a meaning of “point” as a gesture, not a description of comments.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The author’s memoir is unusually ______: she admits her own mistakes as readily as she describes her successes, and she never casts herself as the hero of every story.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['candid', 'self-congratulatory', 'evasive', 'melodramatic'], a: 0, x: 'Openly admitting mistakes and not making herself the hero is honest and frank: **candid**.', w: ['', 'She admits her mistakes and doesn’t make herself the hero, so the memoir isn’t self-congratulatory.', '“Evasive” means avoiding the truth, but she openly admits her mistakes.', '“Melodramatic” means exaggerated, which doesn’t fit an honest account.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 3, ps: 'The garden had been Ruth’s for thirty years, and every plant in it had a story: the rose from her mother’s funeral, the fig grown from a cutting she had carried home in a suitcase. When the new owners arrived, they walked through it quickly, nodding politely, and asked whether the “old bushes” could be cleared to make room for a lawn.', p: Q_PURP, o: ['To show the contrast between the personal meaning the garden holds for Ruth and how it appears to the new owners', 'To describe how to grow a fig tree from a cutting', 'To explain why Ruth decided to sell her house', 'To criticize the new owners for knowing little about plants'], a: 0, x: 'Each plant carries a story for Ruth, while the new owners see only “old bushes”: the text sets these views side by side.', w: ['', 'The fig cutting is part of Ruth’s memories, not a set of instructions.', 'The text never says why Ruth is leaving.', 'The text shows the owners’ view without judging their knowledge of plants.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 3, ps: 'Most coral reefs grow in warm, shallow, sunlit water. ==Yet off the coast of Norway, scientists have mapped vast reefs of cold-water coral growing in darkness hundreds of meters below the surface.== Unlike their tropical relatives, these corals do not depend on sunlight; they feed on tiny organisms carried to them by ocean currents.', p: Q_FUNC, o: ['It presents an exception to a general pattern, which the text then explains.', 'It gives an example of the warm, shallow reefs described in the first sentence.', 'It explains why coral reefs are declining around the world.', 'It describes the methods scientists use to map reefs.'], a: 0, x: 'The first sentence states the usual pattern; the underlined sentence describes reefs that break it, and the last sentence explains how they survive.', w: ['', 'The Norwegian reefs are cold and dark, the opposite of the reefs in the first sentence.', 'Decline isn’t mentioned.', 'Mapping is mentioned, but no methods are described.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 3, ps: '**Text 1**\nMany art historians have credited Johannes Vermeer’s remarkable rendering of light to an extraordinary natural gift, developed through years of careful observation.\n\n**Text 2**\nSome researchers have proposed that Vermeer used optical devices, such as a camera obscura, which projects an image onto a surface, to help him capture subtle effects of light. Other researchers reply that no such devices are mentioned in records of his studio and that his paintings contain features a projected image would not have produced.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 who propose that Vermeer used optical devices most likely respond to the art historians in Text 1?', o: ['By suggesting that Vermeer’s rendering of light may owe something to tools rather than to natural gift alone', 'By arguing that Vermeer was not a skilled painter', 'By agreeing that Vermeer’s rendering of light came entirely from natural gift', 'By pointing out that records of Vermeer’s studio mention a camera obscura'], a: 0, x: 'These researchers think devices helped him capture light, which adds a tool-based explanation to the historians’ “natural gift.”', w: ['', 'Using a tool doesn’t mean he lacked skill; the researchers don’t say that.', 'They propose that devices helped him, so they wouldn’t say it came entirely from natural gift.', 'Text 2 says the records do not mention such devices.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 2, ps: 'Saltwater crocodiles can stay underwater for an hour or more while resting. They manage this partly by slowing their heart rate to just a few beats per minute, which greatly reduces how much oxygen their bodies use.', p: 'According to the text, how are saltwater crocodiles able to stay underwater for so long?', o: ['By slowing their heart rate, which reduces the oxygen their bodies use', 'By breathing through their skin', 'By storing extra air in their tails', 'By swimming to the surface every few minutes'], a: 0, x: 'The text says they slow their heart rate, which cuts their oxygen use.', w: ['', 'Breathing through the skin isn’t mentioned.', 'Storing air in the tail isn’t mentioned.', 'They stay under for an hour or more, so they don’t surface every few minutes.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 3, ps: 'For weeks, Mr. Okafor had told anyone who would listen that the town’s new library was a waste of money. Yet on opening day, it was he who arrived first, wearing his good jacket, and he spent the whole afternoon in the history section, turning the pages of each book with great care.', p: 'Based on the text, what can most reasonably be inferred about Mr. Okafor?', o: ['His behavior on opening day suggests that he values the library more than his complaints implied.', 'He went to the library to find evidence that it was a waste of money.', 'He had been hired to work in the history section.', 'He has never been interested in history.'], a: 0, x: 'Arriving first in his good jacket and handling the books carefully all afternoon shows he cares about the library, despite his complaints.', w: ['', 'His careful, all-day reading suggests enjoyment, not a search for faults.', 'Nothing suggests he works there; he is a visitor.', 'He spends the whole afternoon in the history section, which shows interest.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'Ecologist Mei Lin hypothesizes that the growing population of a certain fish in Clearwater Lake is due mainly to the removal of a dam downstream, which allowed the fish to return from the sea to spawn.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Lin’s hypothesis?', o: ['The fish population grew just as much over the same years in a nearby lake whose downstream dam is still in place.', 'The fish population in Clearwater Lake began to grow the year after the dam was removed.', 'The fish are a popular catch among local anglers.', 'The dam that was removed had been built in 1932.'], a: 0, x: 'If the population grew equally where the dam remains, the dam’s removal probably isn’t the main cause.', w: ['', 'Growth starting right after the removal fits the hypothesis.', 'Fishing popularity doesn’t say what caused the population to grow.', 'The dam’s age doesn’t affect whether its removal caused the growth.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'In a play, a famous singer expresses doubt that her success will last.', p: 'Which quotation from the play most effectively illustrates this claim?', o: ['“They cheer me now, but crowds are fickle; I have watched them cheer the next singer just as loudly.”', '“I have worked for this moment my whole life.”', '“The crowd tonight was the largest I have ever seen.”', '“Success is sweet, and I mean to enjoy every minute of it.”'], a: 0, x: 'Calling crowds fickle and remembering them cheer the next singer shows doubt that her popularity will last.', w: ['', 'This describes her effort, not doubt about the future.', 'This describes her current success without any doubt.', 'This shows confidence and enjoyment, not doubt.'] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 3, ps: 'A researcher claims that in each of three regions, the share of electricity from solar power at least doubled from 2015 to 2022 while the share from coal fell.', ch: { t: 'table', title: 'Share of electricity by source', head: ['Region', 'Solar 2015', 'Solar 2022', 'Coal 2015', 'Coal 2022'], rows: [['North', '4%', '11%', '38%', '29%'], ['Central', '6%', '12%', '45%', '41%'], ['South', '3%', '9%', '30%', '31%']] }, p: 'Which choice best describes data from the table that weaken the researcher’s claim?', o: ['In the South, the share of electricity from coal rose slightly, from 30% to 31%.', 'In the North, the share from solar rose from 4% to 11%.', 'In the Central region, the share from solar exactly doubled, from 6% to 12%.', 'In the North, the share from coal fell from 38% to 29%.'], a: 0, x: 'The claim says coal fell in every region, but in the South it rose, so that weakens the claim.', w: ['', 'Solar more than doubling in the North supports the claim.', 'Solar doubling in the Central region supports the claim.', 'Coal falling in the North supports the claim.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'When a dark object passes behind a star, the star’s brightness does not change. But when an object that gives off its own light passes behind a star and is hidden, the total brightness of the pair drops slightly. Astronomers observing a distant star found a large dip in brightness every 9.3 days, when a companion passes in front of the star, and a much smaller dip exactly halfway between the large ones. This suggests that the companion ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['gives off some light of its own.', 'is larger than the star it orbits.', 'gives off no light at all.', 'orbits the star once every 4.65 days.'], a: 0, x: 'The small dip happens halfway through the orbit, when the companion is behind the star. A drop at that point means the companion’s own light was being hidden.', w: ['', 'The size of the dips doesn’t show that the companion is larger; the large dip happens when it blocks the star.', 'If the companion gave off no light, hiding it behind the star wouldn’t change the brightness.', 'The large dips are 9.3 days apart, so one orbit takes 9.3 days; the small dip comes halfway through it.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'In a study of 400 city apartments, researchers found that residents whose windows faced trees reported less stress than residents whose windows faced other buildings. However, apartments facing trees were also, on average, larger and located on quieter streets. The researchers therefore concluded that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['the view of trees may not be the only reason for the difference in reported stress.', 'a view of trees is the cause of lower stress.', 'larger apartments lead residents to plant more trees.', 'residents whose windows face buildings experience no stress at all.'], a: 0, x: '“However” introduces other differences (size, noise) that could also explain the lower stress, so the researchers can’t credit the trees alone.', w: ['', 'The other differences (size, quiet streets) are exactly why the researchers can’t claim trees are the cause.', 'Nothing suggests residents planted the trees.', 'Those residents reported more stress, not none.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The pianist practiced for six hours a ______ her teacher worried that she might injure her hands.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['day;', 'day,', 'day', 'day, and,'], a: 0, x: 'Both parts are complete sentences, so a semicolon joins them.', w: ['', 'A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together.', 'No comma belongs after “and”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'In 1972, the crew of Apollo 17 photographed Earth from about 29,000 kilometers ______ known as “The Blue Marble,” has become one of the most widely reproduced images in history.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['away; the image,', 'away, the image,', 'away the image,', 'away, and, the image,'], a: 0, x: 'Two complete sentences are joined, so a semicolon is needed. “The image, known as…, has become…” is the second sentence.', w: ['', 'A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, the two sentences run together.', 'No comma belongs after “and”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The only known painting by the artist ______ survived the fire hangs today in a small museum in Ghent.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['that', ', which', ', that', 'which,'], a: 0, x: '“That survived the fire” identifies which painting, so it takes no commas, and nothing separates the subject from “hangs”.', w: ['', '“, which” would need a closing comma before “hangs”, and the clause identifies the painting, so it shouldn’t be set off.', '“That” clauses never take a comma before them.', 'The comma after “which” wrongly splits the clause.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'Many plants defend themselves with ______ stinging nettles, for example, are covered in tiny hollow hairs that inject irritating substances into anything that brushes against them.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['chemicals;', 'chemicals,', 'chemicals', 'chemicals, such as'], a: 0, x: '“Stinging nettles, for example, are covered…” is a complete sentence, so a semicolon is needed between the two sentences.', w: ['', 'A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together.', '“Such as” would call stinging nettles a kind of chemical, and “for example” would repeat the idea.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'The flock of geese ______ south every autumn, stopping at the same lake each year.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['flies', 'fly', 'are flying', 'have flown'], a: 0, x: 'The subject is “flock” (singular), so the verb is “flies”.', w: ['', '“Fly” is plural, but the subject “flock” is singular.', '“Are flying” is plural, but “flock” is singular.', '“Have flown” is plural, but “flock” is singular.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'Each of the paintings, along with several sketches by the same artist, ______ been lent to the museum for the exhibition.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['has', 'have', 'are', 'were'], a: 0, x: 'The subject is “each”, which is singular; “along with several sketches” doesn’t make it plural.', w: ['', '“Have” is plural, but the subject “each” is singular.', '“Are” is plural, and “are been” isn’t a correct verb form.', '“Were” is plural, and “were been” isn’t a correct verb form.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'The scholar ______ the committee chose to lead the project has written three books on the subject.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['whom', 'who', 'which', 'whose'], a: 0, x: 'The pronoun is the object of “chose” (the committee chose her), so the object form “whom” is correct.', w: ['', '“Who” is the subject form, but here the pronoun is the object of “chose”.', '“Which” refers to things, not people.', '“Whose” shows possession, which doesn’t fit here.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'Most metals are solid at room temperature. ______ mercury is a liquid, which is why it was once used in thermometers.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['By contrast,', 'Therefore,', 'For example,', 'In addition,'], a: 0, x: 'Mercury is an exception to the first sentence: a contrast.', w: ['', 'Mercury being liquid isn’t a result of most metals being solid.', 'Mercury is an exception, not an example of a solid metal.', 'Mercury doesn’t add a similar point; it contrasts with the first sentence.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'The study found that students who took notes by hand remembered more of a lecture than students who typed their notes. ______ the handwriting group did better only on a test given a week later; on a test given right after the lecture, the two groups scored about the same.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Specifically,', 'Consequently,', 'Similarly,', 'Instead,'], a: 0, x: 'The second sentence narrows the finding by giving the exact conditions under which it held: “Specifically”.', w: ['', 'The details aren’t a result of the finding; they spell out when it held.', 'The second sentence narrows the first rather than adding a similar point.', '“Instead” would replace the finding, but the second sentence spells it out.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'Rewilding projects aim to restore ecosystems by bringing back species that once lived there. ______ such projects can be controversial: farmers near one reserve objected when wolves were reintroduced, fearing for their livestock.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['That said,', 'Therefore,', 'For instance,', 'In other words,'], a: 0, x: 'After describing the projects’ aim, the writer adds a qualification (they can be controversial): “That said”.', w: ['', 'Being controversial isn’t a result of aiming to restore ecosystems.', 'Controversy isn’t an example of restoring ecosystems.', 'The second sentence adds a new point; it doesn’t restate the first.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- Marie Curie was a physicist and chemist.\n- In 1903, she shared the Nobel Prize in Physics.\n- In 1911, she won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry.\n- She was the first person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize what made Curie’s Nobel Prizes unusual. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Curie, who won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1903 and in Chemistry in 1911, was the first person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.', 'In 1903, Curie shared the Nobel Prize in Physics.', 'Marie Curie was a physicist and chemist.', 'In 1911, Curie won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry.'], a: 0, x: 'What made her prizes unusual is that she was the first to win in two different sciences; only one choice says so.', w: ['', 'This mentions one prize but not what made her prizes unusual.', 'This describes her fields, not what made her prizes unusual.', 'This mentions one prize but not what made her prizes unusual.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.\n- It is often said to have been destroyed in a single great fire.\n- Historians now think it declined gradually over several centuries as its funding fell and scholars left.\n- Some of its collection may have been lost when fires spread through the city in 48 BCE.', p: 'The student wants to challenge a common belief about the library’s end. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Although the Library of Alexandria is often said to have been destroyed in a single fire, historians now think it declined gradually over several centuries.', 'Some of the library’s collection may have been lost when fires spread through the city in 48 BCE.', 'The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.', 'Scholars left the Library of Alexandria as its funding fell.'], a: 0, x: 'Challenging a belief means stating it and then presenting the different view; only one choice does both.', w: ['', 'A fire in 48 BCE fits the common belief more than it challenges it.', 'This describes the library’s size, not its end.', 'This gives one cause of the decline but never states the belief it challenges.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- Two research teams studied how bumblebees choose flowers.\n- Both teams used artificial flowers in a laboratory.\n- Team A found that bees preferred blue flowers.\n- Team B found that bees preferred flowers with a stronger scent, regardless of color.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize that the two teams reached different conclusions. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['While Team A found that bees preferred blue flowers, Team B found that scent mattered more than color.', 'Both teams used artificial flowers in a laboratory.', 'Two research teams studied how bumblebees choose flowers.', 'Team A found that bumblebees preferred blue flowers.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice sets the two different conclusions side by side.', w: ['', 'This is a similarity in method, not a difference in conclusions.', 'This introduces the topic without any conclusions.', 'This gives only one team’s conclusion, so nothing is contrasted.'] }
]);
